#### Article 10

1. Protection of children starts from the womb until the age of 18 (eighteen) years.  
2. Protection of children in the womb, infants and toddlers includes:a. protection of children in the womb, including ensuring children are born in a healthy condition, to reduce maternal mortality rates and reduce child mortality rates;  
b. ensuring every child is born through a medical process in accordance with SOPs from the Ministry of Health;  
c. ensuring every child after birth is conducted with religious guidance professed by their parents;  
d. provision of complete immunization facilities both at posyandu, puskesmas or government hospitals; e. improving the quality and competence of cadres at Posyandu, to ensure correct procedures in order to guarantee the health of mothers and children including the immunization process;  
f. provision of proper facilities for every posyandu and Toddler Family Development (BKB) in every kelurahan; g. ensuring the quality of knowledge of posyandu cadres and toddler family development cadres in accordance with government standards;  
h. providing education to the community about becoming great parents, to ensure child health, both at posyandu, toddler family development together with government and community work partners;  
i. provision of playgrounds and childcare facilities managed by the regional government or organizations under the auspices of the Regional Government;  
j. provision of Breastfeeding Corners in offices that have female workers; andk. provision of attention and affection from the family. (3) Protection for school-age children:a. prohibiting acts of bullying against children at school;  
b. ensuring there are no acts of bullying between children, teachers to children, parents to children and the community to children both at school, at home and in the midst of the community;  
c. providing education to schools, families and the community about child-friendly schools;  
d. requiring every school at all levels to meet child-friendly school criteria;  
e. receiving full attention and affection from family and community;  
f. receiving religious guidance and local customs/culture including sumbang 12 (dua baleh);  
g. receiving twelve years of compulsory education;  
h. providing scholarship programs for underprivileged children and children who excel;  
i. providing supporting facilities for extracurricular activities such as for sports subjects, arts and other local content as well as activity supervisory teachers; andj. prohibiting school children from being in internet cafes during school hours and after 18.00 WIB.  
4. Every person and/or any party is obliged to protect school-age children from acts of violence in the family, school and community environment.  
5. Protection of neglected children:a. regional government, NGOs/social organizations and the community are obliged to provide protection for neglected children domiciled in the Region in accordance with laws and regulations;  
b. the regional government ensures children do not live on the streets;  
c. protection for neglected children whose parents do not have the ability and/or commit neglect as well as children who do not have parents, which is implemented through institutional and non-institutional services by the regional government and the community;  
d. the form of institutional services as referred to in letter (c), is implemented in a family atmosphere and in the child's nearest environment managed by the community and in the form of an institution;  
e. the form of non-institutional services as referred to in letter (c) is implemented in a family atmosphere managed by the community and not in the form of an institution; andf. community-owned institutions as referred to in letter (d) must meet the requirements in accordance with applicable laws and regulations.

# CHAPTER V

## RESPONSIBILITY FOR THE PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN

#### Article 11

The Regional Government together with the community is obliged and responsible to provide support for facilities and infrastructure in the implementation of the protection of women and children.

#### Article 12

The Regional Government guarantees the protection, care, and welfare of children by taking into account their rights and obligations.

#### Article 13

The Regional Government together with the community and the business world are obliged to fulfill facilities and infrastructure in accordance with child-friendly city indicators.

#### Article 14

Families, caregivers, institutions, and customary institutions have the right to take action towards women and children in the context of implementing education, embracing religion, skills development, moral and ethical development, as well as entrepreneurship development for women and children in accordance with religious and customary norms.

#### Article 15

Families, caregivers, institutions and customary institutions are obliged to play an active role in providing the rights of women and children in accordance with religious and customary norms as well as applicable laws and regulations.

# CHAPTER VI

## SYSTEM FOR THE PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN

#### Article 16

1. The protection of women and children is implemented by upholding universal values stated in the Universal Declaration of Human Rights.  
2. Bureaucratic restrictions must not hinder efforts to protect women and children.  
3. Every parent, adoptive parent, guardian, teaching/educational staff, expert, caregiver at orphanages, and leaders of customary law community members are obliged to carry out obligations regarding the rights of children they care for and foster.  
4. Every head of family, head of clan, head of tribe, leader, or other designation of customary law community leader is obliged to fulfill their obligations towards the women in their family, clan and tribe.

#### Article 17

1. The protection of women and children is carried out in the form of protection:a. preventive;  
b. repressive; andc. postremedial.  
2. Preventive protection as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a is implemented through socialization and guidance to parents, heads of families, housewives, guardians, teaching/educational staff, experts, caregivers (owners and officers of orphanages) and customary law communities about their obligations in the protection of women and children.  
3. Repressive protection as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b is implemented through law enforcement by law enforcers to establish and implement legal sanctions against people who violate certain rights of children and women.  
4. Postremedial protection as referred to in paragraph (1) letter c is implemented through efforts to provide assistance in law enforcement by lawyers, psychological guidance by psychologists and or religious leaders, and placement of victims in safe houses.

#### Article 18

The protection of women and children must be carried out by parents, families, relatives, guardians, caregivers, teachers, the Regional Government, and customary law communities in the Region.

# CHAPTER VII

## IMPLEMENTATION OF SOCIALIZATION OF THE PROTECTION

## OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN

#### Article 19

1. The Regional Government is obliged to conduct socialization and education regarding the protection of women and children by involving all elements of customary stakeholders, as well as universities and other community organizations in the Region.  
2. Socialization and education as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out through the implementation of legal counseling, both through lecture methods, discussions, pamphlets, films, plays, randai, saluang, rabab, and other traditional arts.  
3. Financing of programs and activities for socialization and education on the protection of women and children is budgeted in the APBD every year in accordance with regional financial capacity and laws and regulations.
